      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Find all the values of $x$ that satisfy $3x+5>11$ or $2x-3\\le 1.$",
      "worked_solution": "We start by solving $3x+5>11:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+5 & >11 \\\\ 3x & >6 \\\\ x & >2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14237546/assets/87831ea65f4caa557c8e8826faaa43a60737c3590393872902818921668787d0.png)\n\nThen, we solve $2x-3\\le 1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-3 & \\le 1 \\\\ 2x & \\le 4 \\\\ x & \\le 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14237546/assets/4965930532798f18907e5319f2fd9fe5f17d9c1969c12d9795f9867a2cb13351.png)\n\nTo graph the values of $x$ that satisfy either inequality, we overlay the two graphs on the same number line:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14237546/assets/a9543d5a2add249d869a667d16bea75780b0e44e4c0d2bde2cf460733d5e9634.png)\n\nTherefore, the final solution is $x\\le 2$ or $x>2.$ We can write this using interval notation as\n\n$$\nx\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
